    {
      "question": "e-3381",
      "field": null,
      "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
      "old_sha256": "0dfc1a17da85bee7efac2dc3cc32baab873d5f33d73d79b55d88cfd8fd72b106",
      "new_sha256": "39da7328d50ec571ccbacd839cca599ab515ce07c0fcd3897fbfdd4330019941",
      "category": "ma_capture",
      "authoring": [],
      "source": [
        {
          "question": "e-3381",
          "field": null,
          "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
          "value": "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ decreases most rapidly in the direction opposite to that of its gradient.\n\nSo, we compute $-\\nabla f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(2,1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\u2207f(x,y) & =-\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202x}(\\operatorname{ln}\u2061({x}^{2}y))\\text{\u00a0}i-\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202y}(\\operatorname{ln}\u2061({x}^{2}y))\\text{\u00a0}j \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}y}\\cdot 2xy\\text{\u00a0}i-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}y}\\cdot {x}^{2}j \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{x}\\text{\u00a0}i-\\frac{1}{y}\\text{\u00a0}j \\\\ -\u2207f(2,1) & =-\\frac{2}{2}\\text{\u00a0}i-\\frac{1}{1}\\text{\u00a0}j \\\\  & =-i-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function decreases most rapidly in the direction of $- \\, \\mathbf i - \\mathbf j.$",
          "category": "ma_capture",
          "file": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/example-3381.json",
          "file_sha256": "fb6b6c86b31a4ce60405fda2214b9c9207b598810976e988aaa6517afd62ed36"
        }
