    {
      "question": "e-3382",
      "field": null,
      "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
      "old_sha256": "2d33a3a8570ce19c3b1dc8ecee52b88bc763330995eadf8929d780b035f40e72",
      "new_sha256": "439a75e0849bf2102b9eecc56a27df86ccc691a9afe9156fae57fdda218fb577",
      "category": "ma_capture",
      "authoring": [],
      "source": [
        {
          "question": "e-3382",
          "field": null,
          "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
          "value": "At a given point, a function $g(x,y)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nIn our case, we compute $\\nabla g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $\\left(\\dfrac 12, \\dfrac 32 \\right)\\!\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u2207g(x,y) & =\u27e8\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202x}(\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(\\sqrt{x{y}^{3}})),\\,\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202y}(\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(\\sqrt{x{y}^{3}}))\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x{y}^{3}}}\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{3}}{2\\sqrt{x{y}^{3}}},\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x{y}^{3}}}\\cdot \\frac{3x{y}^{2}}{2\\sqrt{x{y}^{3}}}\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8\\frac{1}{2x},\\,\\frac{3}{2y}\u27e9 \\\\ \u2207g(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2}) & =\u27e8\\frac{1}{2\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2})},\\frac{3}{2\\cdot (\\frac{3}{2})}\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e81,1\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $\\left(\\dfrac 12, \\dfrac 32 \\right)$ in the direction of $\\nabla g\\left(\\dfrac 12, \\dfrac 32 \\right)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u2016\u2207g(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2})\u2016 & =\u2016\u27e81,1\u27e9\u2016 \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
          "category": "ma_capture",
          "file": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069662/example-3382.json",
          "file_sha256": "4e464914cb6ae71045d3ab7a37b627f7bc84630eb6db147fbc54bd5c659a3259"
        }
